# id  tag  kind  t_s  alt_km  L_si_W  flag  note
1 - first_light 0.0030 100.000 4.4187e+04 info  L_si first exceeds 1e-6 of its peak. This is just a visibility threshold; the real value depends on your sensor floor and on where the simulation starts (zstart).
2 A ablation_onset 0.8460 86.678 4.8679e+05 stylized  Net heating flux first exceeds radiative loss at T_ablate = 2300 K, so ablation switches on (a kink in the slope). Real onset is gradual over a few km. BELOW this point the model's light is drag work only (no ablated material): treat light above ~87 km as low-confidence. Loosely like the end of Phase 1 / start of Phase 2 in [R22], but the model has no spin, volatile (Na/K) emission or melt physics.
3 Y first_yield 3.7500 40.982 7.9970e+08 physical  Loading rho v^2 reaches S0 = 1.5 MPa. The body starts to flatten (pancake): area, drag and heating all grow, so the light rises faster. Analogue of the onset of fragmentation (Phase 5 / H_f in [R22]).
4 F flare 3.7697 40.666 2.1101e+09 stylized  Light rises x7.8 in 0.12 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: yield gen 0 at 40.9 km (95% of m0); split gen 0 at 40.4 km (94% of m0). Dust groups supply 53% of the rise and 46% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
5 B first_breakup 3.7832 40.466 3.5833e+09 stylized  Spread factor reached f_split = 2: the body breaks into 4 children plus 20 % of its mass as dust. The dust has a large area-to-mass ratio, decelerates and ablates within a fraction of a second, which makes a sharp flare. The instantaneous release is a model simplification. Light grew x4.5 between first yield and this breakup (pancake spreading).
6 F flare 3.9582 37.787 7.4583e+09 stylized  Light rises x1.7 in 0.10 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: yield gen 1 at 38.0 km (33% of m0); split gen 1 at 37.5 km (31% of m0). Dust groups supply 12% of the rise and 28% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
7 P peak 3.9762 37.522 1.0932e+10 physical  Maximum light. It sits ~+2.8 km from the peak of energy deposition (34.8 km) because the luminous efficiency tau(v) falls as the fragments slow (mass-weighted speed here 19.9 km/s).
8 b bump 4.1156 35.501 9.2574e+09 stylized  Small fluctuation, 55% above the local trend; coincides with a split (gen 1) at 35.5 km. The fine structure of this phase is set by the cascade rules, so its pattern is not predictive of a real event.
9 D rolloff 4.3206 32.811 4.0131e+09 physical  Light has fallen to 1/e of the peak 0.34 s after it. Cause: the kinetic energy is being used up (speed 72% of v0, 34% of the mass still in flight) and tau(v) drops with speed. Analogue of Phase 7 (decline until dark flight) in [R22].
10 - drag_overtakes_ablation 4.3836 32.135 2.3670e+09 stylized  Drag work now exceeds the kinetic energy carried off by ablated mass. The ratio ablation/drag is about Lambda v^2/(2 Q Cd), so this happens when the speed falls below ~14 km/s (from the model's heating law).
11 T tail 5.1246 27.309 1.0754e+07 low-confidence  Below 1e-3 of the peak the light is controlled by groups being retired when they slow below v_dark = 3.0 km/s and by the tau taper (2-4 km/s). Do not interpret structure below this level.
12 R retirement_step 5.3496 26.468 2.5721e+06 artifact  A group is removed because it slowed below v_dark = 3.0 km/s; the light drops 17% within 0.03 s. A bookkeeping step, not a physical dimming.
13 R retirement_step 5.3886 26.295 2.0109e+06 artifact  A group is removed because it slowed below v_dark = 3.0 km/s; the light drops 17% within 0.03 s. A bookkeeping step, not a physical dimming.
14 R retirement_step 5.4246 26.071 1.6025e+06 artifact  A group is removed because it slowed below v_dark = 3.0 km/s; the light drops 18% within 0.03 s. A bookkeeping step, not a physical dimming.
